# /etc/default/earlyoom — kill the largest consumer before RAM+swap exhaustion.
#
# Why this exists: on WSL2 the kernel never OOM-kills here. Nothing is cgroup-bounded
# (memory.max=max on every slice), and the VS Code server lives in init.scope, which
# systemd does not resource-control. So exhaustion doesn't kill a process — it stalls
# the 9p transport until WSL gives up and calls reboot(RB_POWER_OFF), taking the whole
# VM with it and leaving no record of the cause. earlyoom supplies the missing victim.
#
#   -m 8,4   SIGTERM at 8% MemAvailable, SIGKILL at 4%
#   -s 10,5  same thresholds against free swap
#   -r 300   memory report every 5 min (journald)
#   --avoid  never pick infrastructure whose death takes the whole session down
#
# Deliberately no --prefer: earlyoom matches against /proc/PID/comm, and the Node
# processes here report comm=MainThread, not "node" — so a '^node$' rule would never
# fire and would give false confidence. Left to its default scoring earlyoom targets
# the largest RSS, which is the runaway tsserver / extension host anyway.
EARLYOOM_ARGS="-m 8,4 -s 10,5 -r 300 --avoid '^(systemd|init|dockerd|containerd|sshd|wslservice|wslinfo)$'"
